WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:03.603
He built his cocaine empire on a foundation riddled with violence,

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:04.104
blood,

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:05.005
and addiction.

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.376
His menacing brutality forever changed a country,

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:13.580
but his dangerous commodity destroyed families and entire communities.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:20.186
This is a story of Pablo Escobar,

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:22.055
the King of Cocaine.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:56.489
For

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:57.257
over a decade,

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:00.493
the name Pablo Escobar was synonymous with cocaine and violence.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:05.799
He revolutionized the industry of drug trafficking and became king of Colombian coke.

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:10.537
Escobar maintained control of his drug empire through intimidation and fear.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:12.372
At the same time,

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:14.941
he fed the poor of his land and built them homes,

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:15.642
schools,

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:16.376
and hospitals.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:24.818
Colombian-born Pablo Escobar is credited with exploiting America's insatiable love and desire for cocaine.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:29.122
Through his marketing strategy of killing anyone who stood in his way,

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:35.695
Escobar transformed Colombia's mom and pop style cocaine industry into a multibillion dollar conglomerate.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.100
Along with his compadres of the Medellin Cartel,

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:46.639
he developed a narcotics distribution network that created a drug empire the likes of which the world had never seen.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:53.513
He successfully shipped tens of thousands of tons of the precious powder into the United States and eventually Europe,

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:57.050
donning him the godfather of cocaine.

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:10.597
Escobar was a very brilliant tactician with a strategy to protect his operations in Colombia and to develop smuggling operations in the United States,

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:15.101
which allowed him to enter more cocaine here than any other trafficker at the time.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:16.336
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:20.106
William Gately spent 21 years as a United States Customs Special Agent.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.142
During that time period,

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:26.146
he was responsible for numerous drug smuggling and money laundering investigations,

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:30.783
including two that specifically affected Pablo Escobar's cocaine empire.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:31.684
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:34.020
Pablo Escobar was a mafia boss.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.789
He was a ruthless drug trafficker.

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:44.564
who probably killed more people than any individual drug trafficker in the history of Colombia.

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:46.533
He was a ruthless killer.

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:52.038
This man aligned himself with narco-terrorism.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:55.175
He placed bombs on airplanes.

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.509
He murdered people.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.045
Pablo Escobar was no folk hero.

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:05.251
He has blood on his hands in Colombia that'll be in their history books for the next century.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:07.120
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.523
Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1,

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.258
1949,

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:17.397
in the cattle farming district southeast of Medellin during Colombia's era of violence.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:23.436
He was the third child of farmer Abel de Jesus and Hermilda,

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:24.871
a primary school teacher.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.173
Two years after his birth,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.844
the family moved to Medellin before moving to a series of villages where Hermilda taught school.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.647
He was a paisa by birth,

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.349
that is an Antiochanian,

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:39.686
and identified very closely with that region,

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:43.089
as many of the people from that part of Colombia do.

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:45.258
It's got a very distinct subculture.

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:50.997
It's one of the reasons that Pablo Escobar never wanted to leave Colombia to flee to other countries.

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:54.467
Escobar was raised with strong traditional Catholic values.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.736
After years of moving about Colombia,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:59.138
the family eventually settled in Envigado,

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:00.340
outside of Medellin.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:07.247
Bruce Bagley is a professor of international studies at the University of Miami in Coral Gables,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:07.680
Florida.

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:14.854
He has lived in Colombia off and on since 1968 and is an expert on Pablo Escobar and narco-terrorism.

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:17.156
He built churches in Colombia with some of the most

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:18.024
The money that he had,

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:23.196
he donated to the church in Medellin and in the environs of Medellin.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:24.764
At the same time,

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:27.600
he saw no contradiction in killing people,

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:37.977
in acting as an extremely ruthless leader of a criminal organization that grew exponentially from the mid-1970s into the

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:39.045
1980s and beyond.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.180
As a young boy,

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:42.982
Escobar developed a love for soccer,

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.451
which he maintained and supported throughout his life.

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:47.587
Pablo Escobar,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.155
who was also a sports fan,

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:52.659
decided to invest heavily in building up Colombian soccer.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:54.794
And he invested in soccer teams,

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.496
particularly in Medellin.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:05:00.933
And he bought the very best talent that he could find everywhere in Latin America.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:02.035
And by the mid and late

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:10.777
1980s, Pablo Escobar and the other members of the Medellin cartel had built Colombian soccer into a world contender.

00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:12.478
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:15.114
He became increasingly discontent and impatient with school.

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:21.287
Escobar was very ambitious and quickly learned that money was the ticket to higher social circles in Colombia.

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:25.058
It was at this time that he began his career as a petty thief,

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.294
stealing tombstones and sanding them down for resale.

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:32.231
He was a very bright child from the beginning.

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:32.899
Indeed,

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:41.007
many of Colombia's major law enforcement officials and ministers have noted that in their conversations with him,

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:43.343
his intelligence was stellar.

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:47.947
Pablo Escobar never attended college in any serious way,

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.916
but he was a high school graduate,

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:51.351
at least he claimed to be.

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:53.519
And he was a very good student.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:54.687
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.289
During the mid-1960s,

00:05:56.489 --> 00:05:59.492
Escobar's hometown of Envigado began to turn violent.

00:06:00.293 --> 00:06:03.162
Gangs emerged along with a thriving marijuana trade.

00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:06.399
While still a teenager,

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:12.672
he began to associate with extortionists and car thieves who gathered in Medellin's seedy Jesus de Nazareno district.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:16.542
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:17.377
By the end of the decade,

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:22.315
Escobar was an accomplished car thief and automobile smuggler with his own gang.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:39.866
Everybody who knew him during his youth commented on his quick intelligence and his capacity to absorb information and to put things together in ways that many other people did not.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.135
That's one of his greatest talents.

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:47.407
It made him an innovative entrepreneur in the clandestine trade of drug trafficking.

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.078
Driven by his desire to become rich,

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:55.982
Escobar's criminal activities escalated to murder for hire.

00:07:01.888 --> 00:07:02.255
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:05.458
Esquivar's rise through Columbia's criminal ranks was swift and violent.

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:09.162
He was young and ambitious,

00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:09.829
but moreover,

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:10.496
he was smart.

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:15.435
He knew what he wanted and would not allow anyone to stand in his way.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:19.505
In less than a decade,

00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:23.810
Escobar had evolved from a petty thief to a cold-blooded contract killer.

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:31.951
He served a brief stint in jail for presenting false documents.

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:36.656
It was his first opportunity to observe hardened prisoners and the lawyers who defended them.

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:39.992
They would later become the core of his organization.

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.331
Fueled by his love of money and fast cars,

00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:51.471
Escobar and his cousin Gustavo Gaviria reportedly smuggled their first load of cocaine in a car race.

00:07:52.038 --> 00:08:02.148
They recognized when Pablo Escobar was in his early 20s that in comparison with stealing cars or bicycles or windshield wipers or things like that,

00:08:02.482 --> 00:08:05.318
that you could make a lot more money trafficking in drugs.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:12.325
Escobar overcame humble beginnings to become the world's wealthiest and most notorious cocaine trafficker.

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:13.893
During the

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:16.863
1970s, coke became a drug of choice for the chic,

00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:17.463
the rich,

00:08:17.463 --> 00:08:18.064
and the famous.

00:08:18.965 --> 00:08:20.833
It was also found to be highly addictive.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:23.503
As a seuss exploded in the US,

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:29.141
Pablo Escobar and the Median cartel positioned themselves to feed the insatiable demand.

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:31.110
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:38.017
The genesis of Pablo Escobar's rise to international infamy began with an airplane flight from Colombia to the United States.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:41.053
In the mid-1970s,

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:44.524
the street value of a kilo of cocaine was $35,000,

00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:47.326
and a small plane could carry millions of dollars'

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:47.593
worth.

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:50.496
The innovation was utilizing airplanes,

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:51.631
dropping off the loads,

00:08:51.764 --> 00:08:53.232
offloading them to cigarette boats,

00:08:53.566 --> 00:08:57.003
and then bringing them into the United States by boat,

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:57.470
into

00:08:57.837 --> 00:08:58.871
South Florida particularly.

00:08:59.272 --> 00:09:04.210
That transportation revolution was one of the major innovations of the Medellin cartel.

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:05.978
ROBERT STACKNELL,

00:09:05.978 --> 00:09:07.146
After Escobar's first flight,

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:14.287
he decommissioned the small plane and mounted it above the entry gate of his farm so that everyone could see he was smuggling cocaine.

00:09:15.521 --> 00:09:15.955
By the late

00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:18.925
1970s, with just a few flights under his belt,

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:21.427
Escobar had already become a millionaire.

00:09:21.427 --> 00:09:23.930
In 1979,

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:27.967
cocaine smuggling would enter an extremely profitable and ultra-violent new era.

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:31.771
Pablo Escobar began a partnership with Carlos later.

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:35.107
Later was allegedly the biggest of the drug kings.

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:36.943
The son of a German engineer,

00:09:37.043 --> 00:09:38.177
he grew up in Colombia.

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:44.584
It was Later who persuaded Escobar to begin smuggling larger shipments of cocaine in larger planes.

00:09:45.151 --> 00:09:47.720
The planes were now equipped with long range fuel tanks,

00:09:47.887 --> 00:09:48.854
radar detectors,

00:09:48.955 --> 00:09:50.656
and communications scramblers.

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:54.260
To avoid American airspace,

00:09:54.594 --> 00:09:55.161
Escobar,

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:55.494
Later,

00:09:55.528 --> 00:10:01.500
and others used the Bahamas as a stopover for breaking down the cocaine shipments before smuggling them into the US.

00:10:03.202 --> 00:10:07.306
They pretty much brought cocaine into the country at will.

00:10:08.007 --> 00:10:08.874
In South Florida,

00:10:09.408 --> 00:10:10.076
for example,

00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:18.884
private aircraft and commercial aircraft were bringing tons of cocaine into the peninsula on a daily basis.

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:20.086
In addition to that,

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.657
hideaways in the Bahama Islands were being used by the Colombians,

00:10:24.724 --> 00:10:26.292
predominantly the Medellin cartel.

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:35.067
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:10:35.067 --> 00:10:38.137
It was during one of their trips to the Bahamas when they met Robert Vesco.

00:10:39.405 --> 00:10:39.739
Vesco,

00:10:39.805 --> 00:10:42.475
who began his career as a machine tool manufacturer,

00:10:42.642 --> 00:10:49.382
was one of the first international criminals to realize that changes had to be made in the movement of money and the shipping of cocaine.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:56.789
The old practice of smuggling large quantities one flight at a time is now considered to be too risky.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:01.494
The big loads were coming across the Atlantic from the Bahamas.

00:11:01.527 --> 00:11:03.629
They were coming in private aircraft,

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:06.799
and they were typically 500 to 1,000 kilos at a time,

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:08.134
and this is an everyday event.

00:11:08.401 --> 00:11:18.144
Entire islands were purchased by traffickers and set up with air bases where they transported cocaine from the mainland of Colombia onto the island.

00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:19.945
Norman's Key Island,

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:21.180
located in the Bahamas,

00:11:21.347 --> 00:11:30.923
was purchased by Escobar and later and was converted into a sinister military-type base equipped with sophisticated communications equipment and navigational aids.

00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:33.025
By the early

00:11:33.392 --> 00:11:38.664
80s, Pablo Escobar was to cocaine trafficking what Bill Gates would soon be to the computer industry.

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:41.934
He was making over half a million dollars a day,

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:47.039
but he had serious competition from the Ochoa brothers and the virulent mafioso gangster,

00:11:47.406 --> 00:11:49.041
José Rodríguez Gacha.

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:55.648
José Gonzalo Rodríguez Gacha was a genius in discovering routes for smuggling into the United States.

00:11:55.881 --> 00:11:58.951
The Ochoas were very good at laundering money.

00:11:59.819 --> 00:12:02.788
Pablo Escobar ran the organization,

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:03.389
if you like,

00:12:03.823 --> 00:12:14.633
and he concentrated in his hands the routes that were developed to bring cocaine through airplane traffic into the United States.

00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:15.234
States.

00:12:15.835 --> 00:12:30.916
um across the caribbean and into south florida and they innovated in a variety of different ways in that route it was together that escobar later and the ochoa brothers and gotcha would spread the wealth and share the risk in the lucrative cocaine industry what

00:12:31.016 --> 00:12:37.389
made him different than any other group was this the first time they were really organized in a cartel type fashion

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:45.898
Special Agent Ramiro Escudero spent 12 years investigating drug trafficking and money laundering for the FBI's Los Angeles Field Division.

00:12:46.165 --> 00:12:49.235
We consume the largest number of drugs anywhere in the world.

00:12:49.468 --> 00:12:53.472
Pablo Escobar was able to take advantage of that and exploit that market.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.041
I think before the Medellin cartel,

00:12:56.041 --> 00:12:58.511
we had a lot of what we call smaller groups.

00:12:58.677 --> 00:12:59.578
Pablo Escobar,

00:13:00.079 --> 00:13:01.914
along with the other members of the Medellin cartel,

00:13:01.947 --> 00:13:04.216
were able to mass market the cocaine.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:05.885
They controlled everything from production.

00:13:06.519 --> 00:13:08.187
to transportation and distribution.

00:13:09.488 --> 00:13:11.524
He became the king of cocaine,

00:13:12.158 --> 00:13:17.129
even though in his own country he had a boss,

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:19.899
a fellow named Jose Rodriguez Gacha.

00:13:21.033 --> 00:13:22.735
But like all bosses,

00:13:22.968 --> 00:13:29.041
if you've got a captain of your industry that produces more wealth for you than anyone else,

00:13:29.041 --> 00:13:30.476
then he's your man.

00:13:32.711 --> 00:13:40.553
A second major innovation of the Medellin Cartel was what I call their Lloyd's of London approach to diversifying the risk.

00:13:41.453 --> 00:13:46.859
If we assume that there are five major participants in the Medellin Cartel in the late 70s and the early 80s,

00:13:47.193 --> 00:13:53.966
what they did was rather than send a single shipment in their own name into the United States running the risk of having it confiscated,

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:58.737
each of the five major capos would share one-fifth of that load.

00:13:59.138 --> 00:14:00.239
So over five loads.

00:14:00.673 --> 00:14:02.374
They would get their entire shipments in.

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:04.743
But if any one of those loads was captured,

00:14:05.044 --> 00:14:06.712
each of them only lost a fifth.

00:14:06.712 --> 00:14:07.079
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:14:08.013 --> 00:14:13.152
The Medellin Cartel was growing stronger and more powerful with every successful shipment into South Florida.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.054
By the beginning of the

00:14:15.855 --> 00:14:21.660
1980s, Miami was engulfed in a full-scale drug war between the newly formed cartel and the Cuban mafia,

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.230
who had controlled Miami's cocaine trade since the 1950s.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:27.399
In 1979 and 1980,

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.835
the so-called cocaine wars that took place,

00:14:30.135 --> 00:14:33.806
here in South Florida that ended up with shootouts on various freeways,

00:14:34.673 --> 00:14:36.475
some 115 people killed.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:43.315
Even with all the indicators that cocaine was becoming a boom business in the United States,

00:14:44.984 --> 00:14:52.725
the government in a law enforcement capacity didn't react to it until South Florida became a Wild West show.

00:14:53.092 --> 00:14:53.926
Won't you join

00:14:54.426 --> 00:14:56.161
Nancy, Vice President?

00:14:56.462 --> 00:15:01.500
Bush and me in urging all America's young people to just say no.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:02.668
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:15:02.701 --> 00:15:09.575
President Ronald Reagan responded by appointing Vice President George Bush to head a special anti-drug task force in South Florida.

00:15:10.009 --> 00:15:11.844
Within a few months,

00:15:13.045 --> 00:15:13.178
a

00:15:14.546 --> 00:15:18.350
500-member task force was in place and functioning in South Florida.

00:15:19.952 --> 00:15:25.391
I was transferred there in early 82 as a supervisor in that task force.

00:15:25.691 --> 00:15:26.825
And in that capacity,

00:15:27.993 --> 00:15:33.299
got to know the cocaine business intimately within a few weeks of my transfer.

00:15:36.402 --> 00:15:39.538
With South Florida's drug trade under the control of the Median cartel,

00:15:40.139 --> 00:15:45.411
Pablo Escobar was now ready to parlay his wealth and power into a position in Colombian politics.

00:15:47.046 --> 00:15:47.947
His perception,

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.649
at least early on by the average Colombian,

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:52.952
was interesting enough a very positive one.

00:15:54.053 --> 00:15:58.524
James Finkenauer is a professor of criminal justice at Rutgers University in New Jersey.

00:15:59.558 --> 00:16:06.065
He was sort of like a Robin Hood character in that what he would do is he would he would use some of his enormous wealth.

00:16:06.432 --> 00:16:10.869
to build housing for the poor and provide other kinds of services for the poor.

00:16:11.503 --> 00:16:11.670
Well,

00:16:11.704 --> 00:16:18.243
Pablo Escobar sought to curry support with the poorer segments of Medellin.

00:16:18.577 --> 00:16:19.778
He recruited many of them,

00:16:20.112 --> 00:16:20.746
gave them jobs,

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:22.781
some as sicarios or gunmen,

00:16:23.248 --> 00:16:27.086
others as employees in various aspects of his business.

00:16:27.186 --> 00:16:35.928
He also sought to curry the loyalty of members of his own organization and those people who worked for him on the various farms that he bought in the Magdalena Medio.

00:16:37.997 --> 00:16:38.297
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:16:38.297 --> 00:16:41.333
Pablo Escobar had the wealth and power to buy anything he wanted.

00:16:41.867 --> 00:16:44.770
And what he wanted now was a political career.

00:16:45.704 --> 00:16:46.271
By the early

00:16:47.039 --> 00:16:47.172
1980s,

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:50.009
Escobar had developed political ambitions.

00:16:50.476 --> 00:16:51.877
And as he became wealthier,

00:16:52.044 --> 00:16:56.015
he began to see a political future for himself.

00:16:56.115 --> 00:16:57.483
And in 1982,

00:16:57.816 --> 00:16:59.451
he ran for the Colombian Congress,

00:17:00.019 --> 00:17:04.757
and he was elected as an alternate deputy to the Colombian lower house.

00:17:06.525 --> 00:17:10.796
Rensselaer Lee is one of the nation's foremost scholars on international drug trafficking.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.232
He is the author of several books,

00:17:13.265 --> 00:17:15.534
including The Andean Cocaine Industry,

00:17:15.768 --> 00:17:20.806
which paints a comprehensive picture of the multibillion dollar South American narcotics business.

00:17:21.073 --> 00:17:22.841
He was embarked on a political career,

00:17:23.142 --> 00:17:24.676
which seemed to be very promising.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:31.917
The trouble was that the money that he got to finance his political career came largely from his activities in the cocaine business.

00:17:32.251 --> 00:17:34.053
But people in Columbia at that time didn't know.

00:17:34.119 --> 00:17:35.788
They thought he was a local entrepreneur.

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:40.859
Escobar's time in the political spotlight would be brief.

00:17:42.127 --> 00:17:45.664
Political enemies and the media sought to reveal the source of his fortune.

00:17:46.632 --> 00:17:46.999
He did,

00:17:47.066 --> 00:17:47.433
I think,

00:17:48.033 --> 00:17:49.768
genuinely care about the,

00:17:50.569 --> 00:17:54.106
about those people who were outside the mainstream of Colombian society.

00:17:54.406 --> 00:17:55.007
And I think he was,

00:17:55.808 --> 00:17:56.775
he would have been able,

00:17:56.909 --> 00:17:57.209
I think,

00:17:57.209 --> 00:18:02.981
to parlay this support among the Colombian poor into a very substantial political career.

00:18:03.482 --> 00:18:06.185
The only trouble was that he was kind of a

00:18:06.518 --> 00:18:08.420
an interloper in the political scene,

00:18:08.854 --> 00:18:14.159
and his political enemies within the Liberal Party and in the media and elsewhere.

00:18:14.393 --> 00:18:16.328
They began to check up on this guy.

00:18:17.629 --> 00:18:18.697
Who is Pablo Escobar?

00:18:18.764 --> 00:18:19.465
Where does he come from?

00:18:19.465 --> 00:18:19.965
And above all,

00:18:19.998 --> 00:18:21.066
where does he get his money?

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:21.967
And gradually,

00:18:22.468 --> 00:18:24.369
they began to go through the legal records.

00:18:24.403 --> 00:18:28.107
They found that he had been arrested for cocaine trafficking back in,

00:18:28.107 --> 00:18:28.307
I think,

00:18:28.307 --> 00:18:29.775
1976.

00:18:30.008 --> 00:18:33.712
ROBERT STACK- The details regarding his arrest were missing from the court's files.

00:18:34.413 --> 00:18:34.713
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:18:34.713 --> 00:18:38.750
The two policemen responsible for the original arrest had been mysteriously murdered.

00:18:39.251 --> 00:18:41.720
The judge who had tried the case could not be found.

00:18:42.788 --> 00:18:47.559
It was clear then that Pablo Escobar had made his fortune through illegitimate means.

00:18:48.727 --> 00:18:54.500
According to people who did know him and one individual who knew him well,

00:18:56.468 --> 00:19:00.005
he was a demanding and ruthless character.

00:19:01.607 --> 00:19:03.675
The Robin Hood image only extended.

00:19:04.977 --> 00:19:09.915
to the PR that he wanted known to the rest of the world.

00:19:09.915 --> 00:19:12.584
ROBERT STACKLIN- In 1983,

00:19:12.584 --> 00:19:18.924
Rodrigo Larrabonilla was appointed as attorney general and launched a campaign to crack down on Colombia's drug trade.

00:19:20.292 --> 00:19:21.093
The following year,

00:19:21.293 --> 00:19:23.028
the police raided Trenquilandia,

00:19:23.195 --> 00:19:25.664
the largest cocaine laboratory ever built.

00:19:27.599 --> 00:19:34.373
As the efforts by the Belisario Betancourt administration towards extradition became more intense,

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:38.610
as the Attorney General of the country at that time,

00:19:38.644 --> 00:19:39.845
Rodrigo Lara Bonilla,

00:19:41.046 --> 00:19:44.650
began to bust the operations of the Medellin Cartel.

00:19:44.783 --> 00:19:45.617
Most importantly,

00:19:46.151 --> 00:19:46.852
Tranquilandia,

00:19:47.119 --> 00:19:50.989
the biggest refining complex ever constructed in Colombia,

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:56.628
capable of producing some thousand kilos of cocaine per month.

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.767
That bust set the Medellin Cartel back in 1984,

00:20:01.767 --> 00:20:03.235
produced a great deal of ire.

00:20:03.468 --> 00:20:04.269
in Pablo Escobar,

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:09.741
and the attorney general was targeted for assassination.

00:20:17.549 --> 00:20:23.288
Escobar ordered the assassination of Bonilla in retaliation for his crackdown against the Median cartel.

00:20:27.826 --> 00:20:32.297
The list of people murdered at the command of Pablo Escobar was becoming long and vast.

00:20:33.732 --> 00:20:33.999
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:20:33.999 --> 00:20:36.134
He would not tolerate anyone standing in his way,

00:20:36.301 --> 00:20:40.372
including law enforcement officials or the Colombian government itself.

00:20:44.142 --> 00:20:45.043
By the mid-'80s,

00:20:45.277 --> 00:20:50.816
Pablo Escobar and the drug lords of Medellin had amassed fortunes rivaling those of big time legitimate businessmen.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:54.786
With his worth estimated at between $2 and $4 billion,

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:59.925
Escobar was written up in Forbes magazine as one of the world's richest men.

00:21:01.193 --> 00:21:04.463
He was able to be an enormously successful businessman.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:05.130
And I mean,

00:21:05.163 --> 00:21:05.697
let's face it,

00:21:05.697 --> 00:21:14.206
the cocaine industry drives a lot of employment and provides a lot of opportunities for people in Colombia who wouldn't otherwise have opportunities.

00:21:14.206 --> 00:21:15.841
And he was a big guy at doing this.

00:21:18.377 --> 00:21:20.879
He was the prince of the capos,

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.415
but they were all in the same ballpark.

00:21:23.548 --> 00:21:30.055
Each of the Medellin cartel barones emerged as a billionaire or a multi-billionaire over the latter part of the

00:21:30.589 --> 00:21:30.722
1980s.

00:21:31.189 --> 00:21:31.923
This is a very,

00:21:32.057 --> 00:21:34.126
very sophisticated business,

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:38.030
the product of which just happened to be cocaine as opposed to

00:21:38.697 --> 00:21:41.033
TV cameras or VCRs or something.

00:21:41.266 --> 00:21:45.137
But the business itself structured very much in the same way.

00:21:45.771 --> 00:21:45.937
And

00:21:46.371 --> 00:21:48.807
Escobar was the head of one of these two businesses,

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:52.678
like the chief executive officer for an international business.

00:21:52.878 --> 00:21:53.578
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:21:53.578 --> 00:21:55.013
Like so many mafia members,

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:57.316
Escobar possessed a certain duality.

00:21:58.016 --> 00:22:01.019
He was a ruthless murdering drug lord wanted by the Colombian

00:22:01.219 --> 00:22:02.587
and United States governments.

00:22:03.622 --> 00:22:04.589
But at the same time,

00:22:04.589 --> 00:22:05.357
he was a husband,

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:06.224
loving father,

00:22:06.224 --> 00:22:08.226
and philanthropist who provided jobs,

00:22:08.393 --> 00:22:09.061
built homes,

00:22:09.127 --> 00:22:09.661
hospitals,

00:22:09.728 --> 00:22:10.262
schools,

00:22:10.629 --> 00:22:13.598
and recreational facilities for the people of his region.

00:22:13.965 --> 00:22:18.236
Family for many of the members of the Medellin and other cartels in Colombia.

00:22:18.537 --> 00:22:19.237
was very important.

00:22:19.671 --> 00:22:20.238
On the other hand,

00:22:20.238 --> 00:22:22.874
he also led a rather licentious life.

00:22:23.975 --> 00:22:26.611
When he was not with his wife or with his family,

00:22:26.778 --> 00:22:31.616
they had riotous parties at his farms and in other places where his wife was not there.

00:22:32.617 --> 00:22:36.321
This became widespread knowledge in Colombia,

00:22:36.722 --> 00:22:45.997
and many people who sought to partake of the huge profits of the drug trade and the immense wealth of Pablo Escobar and others went to these parties.

00:22:46.365 --> 00:22:47.933
Obviously he bought ranches.

00:22:49.167 --> 00:22:51.103
He had lots of accoutrements.

00:22:51.169 --> 00:22:51.903
He had women.

00:22:53.205 --> 00:22:54.072
He had horses.

00:22:54.106 --> 00:22:57.809
He had beautiful fixtures in his homes.

00:22:57.809 --> 00:23:00.178
He had an antique car collection.

00:23:00.178 --> 00:23:01.213
He had all the rest of it.

00:23:01.913 --> 00:23:02.614
He had a zoo,

00:23:02.748 --> 00:23:03.415
for example.

00:23:03.582 --> 00:23:04.349
You could go in there.

00:23:04.383 --> 00:23:05.717
You wouldn't have to pay admission.

00:23:05.751 --> 00:23:07.252
Just go in there and see the animals.

00:23:07.319 --> 00:23:14.726
This was another one of these free services that he was giving to the public to enhance his image as being a...

00:23:15.327 --> 00:23:19.131
a socially responsible person and somebody who cared about the Colombian people.

00:23:19.598 --> 00:23:20.265
In reality,

00:23:20.265 --> 00:23:21.767
he was a savage entrepreneur,

00:23:22.067 --> 00:23:26.405
a cold-blooded killer who earned his fortune in the baneful world of narcotics.

00:23:26.538 --> 00:23:35.080
His drug trafficking empire became so large and so famous that he was a household word by the time 80,

00:23:35.213 --> 00:23:36.415
82 came around.

00:23:36.581 --> 00:23:39.117
Everyone knew who Pablo Escobar was.

00:23:39.851 --> 00:23:43.588
It was like who's Joe Gambino or Paul Castellano.

00:23:44.423 --> 00:23:45.023
They knew Joe.

00:23:45.157 --> 00:23:46.691
they knew who Pablo Escobar was.

00:23:46.691 --> 00:23:47.292
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:23:47.959 --> 00:23:50.662
His menacing product had destroyed the lives of millions.

00:23:51.263 --> 00:23:52.564
At the pinnacle of his power,

00:23:52.764 --> 00:23:56.968
the Medellin cartel was making between $6 and $8 billion a year.

00:23:57.536 --> 00:24:02.073
As a result of their efforts to bring more and more cocaine into the United States,

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:05.076
they made up in volume what they lost in per unit price.

00:24:05.076 --> 00:24:05.677
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:24:05.677 --> 00:24:08.013
Americans were consuming more cocaine than ever,

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:12.117
and Escobar was wanted by both the Colombian and the United States government.

00:24:12.451 --> 00:24:13.819
Pablo Escobar did fear.

00:24:14.486 --> 00:24:16.655
Extradition as all the Medellin capos did.

00:24:17.022 --> 00:24:22.127
They knew full well that extradition to the United States meant a lifelong sentence in jail.

00:24:22.494 --> 00:24:23.962
Carlos Leir was extradited.

00:24:24.062 --> 00:24:28.533
He was given life plus 135 years in Marion,

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:29.034
Illinois.

00:24:29.234 --> 00:24:34.606
Pablo Escobar was intelligent enough to know that if he was ever judged in the United States,

00:24:34.806 --> 00:24:38.543
that the sentence he would get would be two lifetime sentences plus 135 years.

00:24:40.412 --> 00:24:42.547
They formed a group called the extraditables,

00:24:42.814 --> 00:24:43.682
the extraditables.

00:24:43.982 --> 00:24:45.717
And those extraditables launched,

00:24:45.984 --> 00:24:46.485
if you like,

00:24:46.518 --> 00:24:54.192
a terrorist campaign against the Colombian government to prevent them from extraditing the major figures of the Medellin cartel.

00:24:56.194 --> 00:25:03.034
The narco-terrorism conducted by the Medellin cartel bloodied South America's fourth largest country for the next seven years.

00:25:04.035 --> 00:25:07.806
The cartel targeted anyone who supported extradition to the U.S.

00:25:09.908 --> 00:25:10.041
it

00:25:10.308 --> 00:25:13.812
led to the assassination of a variety of major Colombian officials,

00:25:14.145 --> 00:25:14.546
judges,

00:25:14.846 --> 00:25:15.981
police officers,

00:25:17.048 --> 00:25:18.650
notables in Colombian society.

00:25:19.351 --> 00:25:19.951
Guillermo Cano,

00:25:20.151 --> 00:25:22.287
the director of El Espectador newspaper,

00:25:23.121 --> 00:25:23.989
was assassinated.

00:25:24.222 --> 00:25:27.459
El Espectador headquarters were bombed during this campaign,

00:25:27.859 --> 00:25:30.061
and the violence became increasingly indiscriminate.

00:25:32.564 --> 00:25:38.470
He used the revolutionary forces of his fighting against the government of his country to protect his operations.

00:25:39.004 --> 00:25:41.239
Providing them with arms and money,

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:43.241
housing for their families,

00:25:44.276 --> 00:25:46.177
and education for their children,

00:25:46.378 --> 00:25:49.047
churches for them to worship in.

00:25:49.047 --> 00:25:51.383
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:25:51.383 --> 00:25:53.451
It was open season on government officials,

00:25:53.685 --> 00:25:56.121
and one of Escobar's methods of persuading judges,

00:25:56.254 --> 00:25:56.922
politicians,

00:25:56.955 --> 00:25:59.057
and journalists was through intimidation.

00:26:01.526 --> 00:26:05.497
It was common practice for Escobar to send a message through one of his many attorneys.

00:26:08.066 --> 00:26:12.037
He would offer the targeted official a choice between plomo or plata,

00:26:12.671 --> 00:26:14.139
lead or silver.

00:26:16.308 --> 00:26:20.412
The messenger would also arrive with a photo album filled with pictures of the victim's home,

00:26:20.845 --> 00:26:21.212
family,

00:26:21.246 --> 00:26:21.913
and friends.

00:26:23.715 --> 00:26:25.617
In return for his life and cooperation,

00:26:26.117 --> 00:26:28.653
Escobar would pay the official a large sum of money.

00:26:29.888 --> 00:26:30.255
Again,

00:26:30.388 --> 00:26:32.324
because of his power and because of his,

00:26:33.391 --> 00:26:33.992
I would say...

00:26:35.527 --> 00:26:36.461
Just brute force,

00:26:36.461 --> 00:26:38.229
he was able to influence a lot of people.

00:26:38.563 --> 00:26:39.431
The people heard,

00:26:39.664 --> 00:26:41.766
especially judges and prosecutors in Colombia,

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.301
would hear the name Pablo Escobar,

00:26:43.668 --> 00:26:47.105
they would feel like there was a death threat and they would not get in his way.

00:26:48.006 --> 00:26:49.240
In 1985,

00:26:49.541 --> 00:26:52.077
Colombia's murder rate mirrored that of a country at war.

00:26:52.911 --> 00:26:56.648
The city of Medellin alone reported nearly 1,700 homicides,

00:26:57.082 --> 00:26:58.783
the highest per capita in the country.

00:27:00.518 --> 00:27:01.086
No man,

00:27:01.386 --> 00:27:01.653
woman,

00:27:01.686 --> 00:27:06.491
or child was spared in Escobar's campaign of violence against anyone who dared to oppose him.

00:27:07.759 --> 00:27:08.460
One year later,

00:27:08.593 --> 00:27:11.896
the killings in Medellin more than doubled to 3,500,

00:27:11.997 --> 00:27:13.431
nearly 10 murders a day.

00:27:14.132 --> 00:27:14.966
So Pablo Escobar...

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:18.370
became increasingly filled with his own power,

00:27:18.837 --> 00:27:20.538
his own firepower,

00:27:20.672 --> 00:27:21.840
his own political power,

00:27:21.873 --> 00:27:22.974
and his own economic power.

00:27:23.274 --> 00:27:25.176
And he used his billions to,

00:27:25.543 --> 00:27:26.044
if you like,

00:27:26.177 --> 00:27:33.184
break down the doors of the Colombian government and to prevent it from functioning as a law enforcement entity against the Medellin cartel.

00:27:34.352 --> 00:27:36.388
They developed ties with Nicaragua,

00:27:36.655 --> 00:27:38.390
with a number of the Caribbean islands,

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:39.090
with Panama,

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:39.691
of course,

00:27:39.691 --> 00:27:41.393
with General Noriega in Panama,

00:27:42.193 --> 00:27:44.562
with some upper level functionaries in the Castro.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:45.930
government in Cuba,

00:27:47.799 --> 00:27:51.703
and they were very active in many parts of the hemisphere.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:54.139
His cocaine empire was steadily growing,

00:27:54.339 --> 00:27:56.574
and his product was being pushed throughout the world.

00:27:58.209 --> 00:27:58.710
Meanwhile,

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:01.946
the casualties of the drug war in Colombia continued to rise.

00:28:04.382 --> 00:28:08.653
Escobar and his Medellin cartel were responsible for countless assassinations,

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:10.422
including the attorney general,

00:28:10.622 --> 00:28:11.723
the justice minister,

00:28:12.223 --> 00:28:13.291
dozens of journalists,

00:28:13.625 --> 00:28:17.529
More than 200 judges and over a thousand police officers.

00:28:18.463 --> 00:28:31.976
The drug war escalated with the assassination of liberal presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galan in August 1989.

00:28:31.976 --> 00:28:40.585
The terrorist activity sponsored by the Medellin Cartel and particularly Pablo Escobar eliminated three of the six presidential candidates running in the 1990 elections.

00:28:41.019 --> 00:28:42.120
Three were assassinated.

00:28:42.554 --> 00:28:43.121
Out of the six,

00:28:43.421 --> 00:28:44.389
President Gaviria,

00:28:44.522 --> 00:28:45.123
Cesar Gaviria,

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:51.429
was elected because the man who had been picked by the Liberal Party to be their candidate,

00:28:51.463 --> 00:28:52.297
Luis Carlos Galan,

00:28:52.564 --> 00:28:58.970
was assassinated at Pablo Escobar's orders in August of 1989.

00:28:59.037 --> 00:29:03.775
Pablo Escobar's relationship with the Colombian government changed the face of Colombian politics.

00:29:04.075 --> 00:29:06.044
It changed who was president of Colombia.

00:29:06.377 --> 00:29:07.912
After the execution of Galan,

00:29:08.246 --> 00:29:11.583
the Colombian government intensified his campaign to bring down.

00:29:12.050 --> 00:29:12.484
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:29:12.484 --> 00:29:13.084
Medellin Cartel.

00:29:15.453 --> 00:29:17.689
Hundreds of airplanes and vehicles were impounded.

00:29:18.123 --> 00:29:21.893
Thousands of weapons and 25,000 rounds of ammunition were seized.

00:29:22.827 --> 00:29:25.897
Personal property and commercial real estate was confiscated.

00:29:27.265 --> 00:29:31.302
The cartel responded by burning or bombing the homes of numerous politicians.

00:29:32.137 --> 00:29:38.910
But the ultimate example of Escobar's diabolical disregard for life was demonstrated in November of that same year.

00:29:40.678 --> 00:29:41.146
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:29:41.146 --> 00:29:46.351
Over 100 passengers prepared to board the Avianca 727 jet from Bogota to Cali.

00:29:47.552 --> 00:29:52.557
They had no idea that five of their co-passengers were informants against the Medellin Cartel.

00:29:53.825 --> 00:29:54.325
Reportedly,

00:29:54.359 --> 00:29:57.462
they were responsible for the raid of Escobar's jungle hideaway,

00:29:57.796 --> 00:30:00.732
resulting in the kingpin's narrow escape through a cornfield.

00:30:01.833 --> 00:30:06.971
The six crew members prepared for takeoff while the unsuspecting passengers settled in for the flight.

00:30:08.072 --> 00:30:09.707
The plane ascended into the air.

00:30:12.443 --> 00:30:12.877
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:30:12.877 --> 00:30:13.678
But en route to Cali,

00:30:13.678 --> 00:30:15.213
the plane exploded in midair,

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:16.648
killing everyone aboard.

00:30:26.357 --> 00:30:28.459
Pablo Escobar had struck again.

00:30:33.598 --> 00:30:37.202
The charred remains of the Avianca plane were broadcast throughout the world.

00:30:40.338 --> 00:30:40.772
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:30:40.772 --> 00:30:44.542
There seemed to be no escaping the menacing terror inflicted by the powerful cartel.

00:30:46.711 --> 00:30:51.583
The public's cry to reach a compromise between the government and the drug lords was louder than ever.

00:30:55.520 --> 00:31:01.259
The continuing war between the Colombian government and the Median cartel was beginning to take its toll on the cocaine empire.

00:31:02.093 --> 00:31:03.027
For over a decade,

00:31:03.161 --> 00:31:06.965
Pablo Escobar had exerted his might in Colombia through bribery and murder.

00:31:08.399 --> 00:31:08.666
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:31:08.666 --> 00:31:09.434
During the beginning of the

00:31:10.201 --> 00:31:17.775
1990s, Escobar found himself enthralled in an intensifying campaign conducted by Colombia and the United States to bring him down.

00:31:19.143 --> 00:31:19.577
Ultimately,

00:31:20.144 --> 00:31:22.580
Escobar became enemy number one.

00:31:23.648 --> 00:31:25.550
The Medellin Cartel became a target.

00:31:25.617 --> 00:31:31.923
Colombia began to believe that their national security depended on the destruction of the Medellin Cartel,

00:31:31.923 --> 00:31:35.193
and they went about doing this with a certain amount of help from the United States.

00:31:35.393 --> 00:31:36.060
So we were,

00:31:36.327 --> 00:31:36.895
as a government,

00:31:36.895 --> 00:31:38.363
aware of his power,

00:31:38.463 --> 00:31:43.568
and we were trying to assist Colombia in either apprehending him or extraditing him to the United States.

00:31:45.336 --> 00:31:50.074
He saw an opportunity to strike a bargain with the Colombian government to,

00:31:50.408 --> 00:31:50.909
in effect,

00:31:51.276 --> 00:31:53.211
enter into a plea bargaining situation,

00:31:53.478 --> 00:31:54.946
which would reduce his sentence,

00:31:55.146 --> 00:31:57.282
eliminate the possibilities of his extradition,

00:31:57.615 --> 00:31:58.950
and give him a few years in jail,

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:02.053
and he would emerge with his money more or less intact,

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:03.221
his billions intact.

00:32:03.788 --> 00:32:06.925
With a guarantee that he would not be extradited to the United States,

00:32:07.258 --> 00:32:11.496
Escobar turned himself in under the terms he had dictated to the Colombian government.

00:32:13.131 --> 00:32:13.631
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:32:13.631 --> 00:32:13.898
He financed,

00:32:13.932 --> 00:32:14.365
designed,

00:32:14.365 --> 00:32:17.969
and built his own jail named La Cathedral outside of Medellin.

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:27.011
It's very hard for the head of a criminal organization to continue to influence that organization in the same manner when they're in prison.

00:32:27.412 --> 00:32:28.880
You can do it for a little while,

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:33.051
but the troops begin to get restless,

00:32:33.885 --> 00:32:36.888
and you're not there anymore to enforce these rules,

00:32:36.888 --> 00:32:38.389
and I think that happened with Escobar.

00:32:39.157 --> 00:32:42.827
He felt that he could have the best of all possible worlds.

00:32:43.161 --> 00:32:45.563
He had many people at that time who were after him,

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.065
not just the Colombian government.

00:32:47.065 --> 00:32:49.067
He had his enemies in the Cali Cartel.

00:32:49.867 --> 00:32:52.670
He had a falling out with some people within the Medellin Coalition.

00:32:53.538 --> 00:33:00.511
And I think he wanted to come into some secure facility where he could rest and relax,

00:33:00.678 --> 00:33:03.414
and at the same time continue to run his cocaine empire.

00:33:03.681 --> 00:33:04.582
He had his own cooks.

00:33:04.916 --> 00:33:06.217
He had visitors come in.

00:33:06.417 --> 00:33:09.854
The fence around it kept the military out and kept his rivals out.

00:33:10.154 --> 00:33:11.589
It didn't necessarily keep him in.

00:33:13.825 --> 00:33:17.395
The prison facility that he built was to his specifications.

00:33:21.799 --> 00:33:22.333
From there,

00:33:22.967 --> 00:33:26.671
he was able to manage his cocaine empire.

00:33:26.738 --> 00:33:27.939
He had computers,

00:33:27.972 --> 00:33:29.173
he had fax machines,

00:33:29.207 --> 00:33:30.108
he had telephones,

00:33:30.108 --> 00:33:33.344
he had everything that he needed to communicate with the outside world.

00:33:33.611 --> 00:33:34.012
And also,

00:33:35.113 --> 00:33:36.647
he used the prison not just...

00:33:37.081 --> 00:33:39.917
to manage his cocaine trafficking operations,

00:33:40.451 --> 00:33:43.755
but also to conduct trials,

00:33:44.122 --> 00:33:47.825
summary judgments against his enemies within the Medellin Cartel.

00:33:48.593 --> 00:33:50.294
Some people who went there to see him,

00:33:50.528 --> 00:33:51.029
talk to him...

00:33:51.662 --> 00:33:53.464
never seen alive again.

00:33:54.065 --> 00:33:56.200
So all kinds of things went on in that prison.

00:33:57.135 --> 00:33:59.804
Escobar was also able to leave the prison at will.

00:34:00.972 --> 00:34:01.506
He would leave,

00:34:01.506 --> 00:34:02.140
for example,

00:34:02.140 --> 00:34:06.811
on a Saturday night to go down to Envigado and go to a local bar and have drinks with his friends,

00:34:06.844 --> 00:34:07.612
have a good time.

00:34:08.546 --> 00:34:10.048
He was seen at soccer matches,

00:34:10.515 --> 00:34:11.315
football matches.

00:34:11.682 --> 00:34:13.684
He really had quite a good life there,

00:34:13.885 --> 00:34:20.892
until finally it became so obvious what he was doing that the government decided they were going to move him to a military prison,

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:21.492
which would have

00:34:21.492 --> 00:34:23.361
have been considerably less comfortable.

00:34:23.594 --> 00:34:25.229
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:34:25.263 --> 00:34:27.698
Rather than serve out his time in a military prison,

00:34:27.932 --> 00:34:34.205
Escobar fled La Catedral and lived out the remainder of his life as a fugitive on the run from his numerous enemies.

00:34:35.473 --> 00:34:38.609
Escobar had built for himself a protection network,

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:41.279
not just his support structure in Medellin.

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:42.780
Among the poor,

00:34:43.181 --> 00:34:45.349
he also paid officials,

00:34:45.616 --> 00:34:46.484
paid policemen,

00:34:46.684 --> 00:34:47.985
paid army officers,

00:34:48.386 --> 00:34:49.754
paid other people authority.

00:34:51.289 --> 00:34:57.562
who to tell him when a police raid or dragnet was going to take place,

00:34:57.829 --> 00:34:58.096
where,

00:34:58.563 --> 00:34:59.630
so he could get out of the way.

00:35:00.231 --> 00:35:02.400
Escobar made many miraculous escapes,

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.670
but his reign as a king of cocaine had come to an end.

00:35:06.871 --> 00:35:11.209
The Rodriguez-Arguera brothers of Cali had gained ground in the drug trafficking industry.

00:35:12.143 --> 00:35:17.048
They were now in position to become the dominant figures in the lucrative yet dangerous cocaine business.

00:35:18.082 --> 00:35:23.054
The Rodriguez-Orgehuelas emerged as the principal figures in Cali,

00:35:23.621 --> 00:35:25.756
rapidly surfaced over the latter part of the

00:35:26.157 --> 00:35:27.058
1980s and the early part of the 90s.

00:35:27.525 --> 00:35:49.046
1990s as the principal traffickers in colombia supplanting medellin and and uh pablo escobar precisely because pablo escobar got increasingly caught up in his own survival and in his terrorist war against the colombian government escobar

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:53.985
was on the run and his median drug cartel was a mere shell of his former narco glory days

00:35:54.886 --> 00:35:55.153
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:35:55.153 --> 00:36:00.892
The ravishing effects of the cocaine war had taken its toll on two continents and exposed Escobar as a diabolical,

00:36:00.992 --> 00:36:04.428
ruthless killer who would do anything to push his lethal product.

00:36:06.464 --> 00:36:11.769
Escobar's escape from his luxurious prison was the ultimate slap in the face to the political establishment.

00:36:14.505 --> 00:36:16.307
With the backing of the American president,

00:36:16.574 --> 00:36:21.779
a special Colombian task force was created for the sole purpose of apprehending Escobar.

00:36:22.713 --> 00:36:25.283
We intend to see them dismantled and destroyed.

00:36:26.784 --> 00:36:33.758
Pablo Escobar had escape routes that allowed him to survive in clandestinity for a lengthy period of time,

00:36:33.958 --> 00:36:35.259
for a couple of years actually.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:38.329
But this was a kind of desperate existence.

00:36:38.663 --> 00:36:43.935
The reality was that he had created an organization with loyal followers,

00:36:44.001 --> 00:36:46.237
both in the Magdalena media where he had a number of farms,

00:36:46.537 --> 00:36:49.106
and throughout Medellin where he had a number of different residences.

00:36:49.674 --> 00:36:50.107
First of all,

00:36:50.107 --> 00:36:51.309
he has a lot of friends in Medellin.

00:36:52.910 --> 00:36:53.377
In other words,

00:36:53.377 --> 00:36:59.050
he has this huge political backing from a lot of the poor people in that city.

00:37:00.384 --> 00:37:01.586
And so there's a certain loyalty,

00:37:01.852 --> 00:37:07.658
and he can depend on this loyalty to provide a certain amount of cover or protection.

00:37:08.559 --> 00:37:09.026
So in other words,

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:15.900
there are people who would not turn him in because they think he's almost like a deity of some sort.

00:37:16.667 --> 00:37:18.169
But his enemies were closing in.

00:37:18.169 --> 00:37:21.739
In 1992 and 1993,

00:37:22.073 --> 00:37:27.478
the Colombian authorities were making a tremendous effort just to demolish that organization.

00:37:28.012 --> 00:37:28.246
I mean,

00:37:28.246 --> 00:37:29.213
they arrested people.

00:37:29.247 --> 00:37:33.150
They killed something like 200 top-ranking and middle-ranking leaders.

00:37:33.684 --> 00:37:35.920
They offered surrender opportunities,

00:37:35.987 --> 00:37:38.556
plea bargain chances for people to come in.

00:37:39.357 --> 00:37:39.790
Basically,

00:37:39.824 --> 00:37:41.759
they put the Medellin cartel out of business.

00:37:42.159 --> 00:37:43.060
It became more costly.

00:37:43.694 --> 00:37:45.162
People simply didn't stay bought.

00:37:45.630 --> 00:37:46.330
and the Colombian

00:37:46.464 --> 00:37:53.704
government with the backing of the United States created new units within both the police and the military in order to pursue them.

00:37:53.704 --> 00:38:00.278
The Bloque de Busqueda was a new unit created within the Colombian military precisely to go after Pablo Escobar.

00:38:00.611 --> 00:38:00.945
Hence,

00:38:01.279 --> 00:38:03.447
they were not able to get to this group.

00:38:03.614 --> 00:38:08.219
They also rotated many of the people that he had previously bought out of Medellin and brought new ones in.

00:38:08.419 --> 00:38:12.390
So we either have to bribe them or kill them in order to gain their loyalty.

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:13.024
ROBERT STACK,

00:38:13.024 --> 00:38:16.193
Colombian President Gaviria intensified the manhunt with a

00:38:16.193 --> 00:38:17.828
with additional command coordination,

00:38:18.462 --> 00:38:20.031
electronic bugging interdiction,

00:38:20.264 --> 00:38:20.364
and

00:38:21.098 --> 00:38:22.033
24-hour surveillance.

00:38:22.900 --> 00:38:24.402
United States assistance was essential,

00:38:24.568 --> 00:38:27.104
but the Gaviria government was committed to getting Pablo Escobar,

00:38:27.171 --> 00:38:27.338
too,

00:38:27.605 --> 00:38:28.939
after he escaped from La Catedral.

00:38:29.106 --> 00:38:33.344
That was a black eye against the Colombian government and a violation of the agreement that they had reached.

00:38:33.544 --> 00:38:37.148
When Pablo Escobar did that and then went out and killed a bunch of members of his own organization,

00:38:37.481 --> 00:38:39.717
really the death bell was ringing already.

00:38:39.917 --> 00:38:42.286
ROBERT STACK- Escobar had been on the run for over a year,

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:44.422
but his luck was about to run out.

00:38:45.389 --> 00:38:45.690
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:38:45.690 --> 00:38:48.292
He had taken refuge in an apartment located in Medellin.

00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:51.595
While talking to his son on his cellular telephone,

00:38:52.029 --> 00:38:54.899
the special police were able to identify his location.

00:38:55.099 --> 00:38:59.036
They were able to triangulate these communications and focus in on him,

00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:01.038
and found the block,

00:39:01.072 --> 00:39:06.677
and finally found the apartment complex and the specific place where he was.

00:39:06.711 --> 00:39:08.312
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:39:08.312 --> 00:39:12.917
Escobar attempted to escape by running across the rooftop of the apartment in which he sought shelter.

00:39:15.052 --> 00:39:15.353
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:39:15.353 --> 00:39:18.622
A barrage of gunfire was exchanged between Escobar and the police.

00:39:20.958 --> 00:39:25.896
Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was gunned down by the special police force on December 2,

00:39:25.896 --> 00:39:27.198
1993,

00:39:28.099 --> 00:39:28.899
one day after his

00:39:29.500 --> 00:39:30.134
44th birthday.

00:39:31.535 --> 00:39:32.136
In the end,

00:39:32.770 --> 00:39:37.208
Escobar lay dead in a pool of his own blood without any shoes on his feet.

00:39:39.009 --> 00:39:43.247
The police celebrated in the streets while his family openly mourned his murder.

00:39:46.183 --> 00:39:47.952
He died a bloody death.

00:39:47.985 --> 00:39:48.753
Because at that point,

00:39:49.320 --> 00:39:51.122
this support structure,

00:39:51.155 --> 00:39:52.189
if you want to call it that,

00:39:53.491 --> 00:39:54.692
protection from above,

00:39:55.359 --> 00:39:56.193
among officials,

00:39:56.293 --> 00:39:56.660
police,

00:39:56.694 --> 00:39:57.395
and military,

00:39:58.062 --> 00:40:02.633
and among his local coteries of supporters in Medellin,

00:40:03.033 --> 00:40:04.468
that had begun to unravel.

00:40:05.035 --> 00:40:07.571
And when somebody becomes a public enemy like he was,

00:40:08.038 --> 00:40:11.976
all of this is going to begin to disintegrate.

00:40:12.576 --> 00:40:13.177
And it was that,

00:40:13.377 --> 00:40:13.711
I think,

00:40:13.811 --> 00:40:17.782
that created the conditions that allowed the police.

00:40:18.315 --> 00:40:20.384
or the so-called bloca de puscata,

00:40:20.618 --> 00:40:21.385
the search block,

00:40:21.952 --> 00:40:22.319
to kill him.

00:40:22.319 --> 00:40:24.221
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:40:26.023 --> 00:40:26.791
Like his life,

00:40:27.291 --> 00:40:36.200
Escobar's funeral was a combination of joy from those who had personally suffered from his terror and sorrow by those who had benefited from his narco wealth and kindness.

00:40:39.703 --> 00:40:42.440
The Medellin cartel had been systematically dismembered.

00:40:43.174 --> 00:40:44.508
Carlos later was in jail.

00:40:45.509 --> 00:40:51.015
Jose Gonzalo Rodriguez Gacha was gunned down in the sugar cane fields along the Colombian coast.

00:40:51.816 --> 00:40:55.386
And the Ochoa brothers all copped plea bargains and were sent to prison.

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:59.457
The underbosses of that organization did not disappear.

00:40:59.557 --> 00:41:00.891
Some of them were thrown in jail as well,

00:41:01.292 --> 00:41:03.327
but many of them continue to operate on their own.

00:41:03.727 --> 00:41:06.330
And so we have remnants of the Medellin Cartel.

00:41:08.632 --> 00:41:08.999
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:41:08.999 --> 00:41:12.436
Years after the death of Pablo Escobar and the demise of his Median cartel,

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:16.207
the Colombian narcotics trade is stronger than ever.

00:41:17.475 --> 00:41:19.910
The large cartel structure has given way to smaller,

00:41:19.977 --> 00:41:23.247
more efficient organizations that not only traffic cocaine,

00:41:23.514 --> 00:41:24.748
but heroin as well.

00:41:25.282 --> 00:41:26.684
So the figurehead has changed.

00:41:26.717 --> 00:41:27.818
Pablo Escobar is gone,

00:41:27.985 --> 00:41:29.286
but we still have a drug problem.

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:30.654
What has changed,

00:41:31.922 --> 00:41:32.723
for the most part,

00:41:33.991 --> 00:41:36.560
is the will in Colombia to do something,

00:41:37.261 --> 00:41:43.133
to salvage their country from a guerrilla force which has aligned itself so well.

00:41:44.435 --> 00:41:45.636
with the drug traffickers.

00:41:46.036 --> 00:41:46.837
They are not a cartel.

00:41:47.137 --> 00:41:48.672
They do not traffic outside of Colombia.

00:41:49.106 --> 00:41:50.641
They're only personally involved in refining.

00:41:50.841 --> 00:41:51.442
But nonetheless,

00:41:51.609 --> 00:41:53.444
they provide protection for landing strips,

00:41:53.477 --> 00:41:54.645
protection for laboratories.

00:41:54.845 --> 00:41:58.682
They collect taxes from the peasant growers of both coca leaf and opium poppy.

00:42:05.523 --> 00:42:09.727
United States citizens use more illicit drugs than any other country in the world.

00:42:10.694 --> 00:42:16.166
Escobar recognized this and capitalized upon the American consumers craving for cocaine.

00:42:17.434 --> 00:42:20.004
We don't seem to understand that this is a huge industry.

00:42:20.671 --> 00:42:25.776
This is an industry that makes a tremendous amount of difference in the economies and the lives of people in South America.

00:42:26.777 --> 00:42:29.013
We can't just eradicate this industry overnight.

00:42:29.380 --> 00:42:31.048
And every time we take steps against it,

00:42:31.048 --> 00:42:31.949
we find that it's not just a industry.

00:42:32.016 --> 00:42:34.518
find that something else crops up in its place.

00:42:34.652 --> 00:42:39.189
There are more people leaving Colombia today than any time in the history of the country,

00:42:40.157 --> 00:42:43.894
because they no longer believe that they can win the war.

00:42:43.928 --> 00:42:45.829
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:42:45.896 --> 00:42:49.400
The legacy of Pablo Escobar will be felt for generations to come.

00:42:51.101 --> 00:42:53.904
Until the demand for illegal drugs is neutralized,

00:42:54.338 --> 00:42:59.209
there will always be a person or an entity willing to satisfy that desire.

00:42:59.643 --> 00:43:08.185
I think most of all what I would remember Pablo Escobar for is for being the major architect of this cocaine industry,

00:43:08.786 --> 00:43:11.055
one of the most important,

00:43:12.289 --> 00:43:13.090
most baneful,

00:43:13.357 --> 00:43:18.762
but also most important industries that has developed in Latin America and really the world.

00:43:19.997 --> 00:43:23.400
Regardless of what he did that was good,

00:43:24.101 --> 00:43:25.102
and perceived to be good,

00:43:25.869 --> 00:43:38.148
He did it for the proliferation of a criminal organization to bring billions in drug profits back to him personally so he could send more poison out.

00:43:39.350 --> 00:43:46.957
I don't believe that a country outside of Colombia knows the devastation of drug trafficking like they do.

00:43:47.791 --> 00:43:49.560
He was born as Pablo Escobar.

00:43:50.094 --> 00:43:53.030
He died the godfather of cocaine.

00:43:55.666 --> 00:43:57.434
Years after Pablo Escobar's death,

00:43:57.768 --> 00:44:00.371
people in Medellin still bring flowers to his gravesite.

00:44:01.405 --> 00:44:01.805
If asked,

00:44:01.805 --> 00:44:03.774
they might tell you about the home he built for them,

00:44:03.841 --> 00:44:06.443
or the food he distributed within their impoverished village,

00:44:06.477 --> 00:44:08.345
or the hospital he constructed for the sick.

00:44:09.813 --> 00:44:12.282
But for the relatives of the Colombian police officers,

00:44:12.383 --> 00:44:12.883
journalists,

00:44:12.916 --> 00:44:14.818
and government officials he assassinated,

00:44:15.386 --> 00:44:16.553
the reply will be different.

00:44:17.888 --> 00:44:19.623
They will tell you of the Pablo Escobar,

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:20.424
who was a ruthless,

00:44:20.557 --> 00:44:21.525
cold-blooded killer,

00:44:22.192 --> 00:44:23.260
who seemed to have it all,

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:24.061
but in the end.

00:44:24.795 --> 00:44:25.329
died alone.

00:44:26.697 --> 00:44:27.431
I'm Robert Stack.

00:44:28.098 --> 00:44:28.932
Thanks for joining us.

